.Dd October 5, 2026
.Dt CONCHA-COMPLETE 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm concha-complete
.Nd how a program tells concha to complete its command line
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/concha/complete/ Ns Ar program
.br
.Pa $XDG_DATA_DIRS/concha/complete/ Ns Ar program
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A completion file describes the subcommands, options and operands of
one program, so that
.Xr concha 1
can complete its arguments with
.Ic Tab
without ever running it.
Nothing in the file can run anything: completion starts no process,
reaches no network and waits on nothing.
Anything that must be computed when
.Ic Tab
is pressed, such as git branches, belongs in a
.Ic complete
function instead, as
.Sx Programmable completion
in
.Xr concha 1
describes.
.Pp
Programs that use
.Xr getopt 3
write their file at
.Ql make install ;
anyone may write one by hand.
.Ss Where concha looks
For an argument of a command, once the user's
.Ic complete
function has returned nothing,
.Nm concha
looks for a file named after the last path component of the command
word: first in
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/concha/complete
.Po
.Pa ~/.local/share/concha/complete
when
.Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset or empty
.Pc ,
then in
.Pa concha/complete
under each directory of
.Ev XDG_DATA_DIRS
.Po
.Pa /usr/local/share:/usr/share
when it is unset or empty
.Pc .
Directories that are not absolute are passed over.
The first file it can read is used: a regular file of at most 64 KB
whose first line is
.Ql complete 1 .
If there is none, the usual rules apply.
The file is read again on every
.Ic Tab ,
so a change takes effect at once.
.Ss Lines
The first line is
.Ql complete 1 ,
the version of the format.
Each line after it is a keyword and its fields, separated by blanks
(spaces and tabs).
A
.Ql #
at the start of a field begins a comment, which runs to the end of the
line; blank lines are ignored.
A
.Ql \e
makes the next character part of the field, so a field may hold a
blank or start with a
.Ql # ;
that is the only quoting.
.Pp
The second field of every line is its
.Em scope ,
which says where on the command line it applies:
.Bl -tag -width "remote.addXX" -offset indent
.It Cm -
the command itself, before any subcommand;
.It Cm start
after the subcommand
.Cm start ;
.It Cm remote.add
after
.Cm remote add .
.El
.Ss Keywords
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm sub Ar scope name ...
The subcommands of
.Ar scope .
.It Cm opt Ar scope short long Op Ar kind ...
An option.
.Ar short
is its letter and
.Ar long
its name without the dashes; either may be
.Ql -
for none.
With no
.Ar kind
it is a flag; with one it takes a value of that kind.
.It Cm arg Ar scope n kind ...
The kind of the
.Ar n Ns th
operand of
.Ar scope ,
counting from 1.
Options and their values are not counted, and neither is the
subcommand word itself.
An operand with no
.Cm arg
line is a
.Cm file .
.El
.Ss Kinds
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm file
File names, as
.Nm concha
completes them anyway.
.It Cm dir
Directories only.
.It Cm word
Free text: nothing is offered, and there is no falling back to file
names.
.It Cm one Ar word ...
One of these words.
.It Cm in Ar path
The names in the directory
.Ar path .
Names starting with a dot are offered only when the word does, and a
missing directory offers nothing.
Only here are
.Ql ~ ,
.Ql $ Ns Ar NAME
and
.Ql ${ Ns Ar NAME Ns }
expanded, from the environment; if one of them is unset or empty,
nothing is offered.
There are no expressions and no backticks.
.It Cm command
This operand and everything after it are a command line of their own,
completed as if typed at the prompt: the program's name first, then
that program's arguments, by its own completion file, the user's
.Ic complete
function, or file names.
.El
.Ss How the line is read
.Nm concha
reads the words from the second up to the one before the cursor.
A word that names a
.Cm sub
of the current scope, before any operand in it, enters that
subcommand's scope.
A word starting with
.Ql -
is an option, until
.Ql --
ends the options; a lone
.Ql -
is an operand.
A group of short flags such as
.Ql -lw
is read letter by letter, and its last letter may take the next word as
its value; a letter that takes a value before the last has the rest of
the word as its value.
.Ql --name=value
is whole in itself.
Every other word is an operand.
.Pp
The word under the cursor then completes to:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
the kind of the option before it, when that option is waiting for its
value;
.It
the kind of the option
.Ar name ,
for the part after
.Ql --name= ;
.It
the scope's options, long as
.Ql --name
and short as
.Ql -x ,
for a word starting with
.Ql -
before any
.Ql -- ;
.It
otherwise, the scope's subcommands, if no operand has come yet,
together with the next operand's kind.
.El
.Pp
As always, a name that starts with the word is offered, and if none
does, names holding its letters in order are.
.Ss How the format grows
A line with a keyword
.Nm concha
does not know is skipped, so new keywords do not break an older
.Nm concha .
An unknown kind is read as
.Cm file .
Extra fields at the end of a line are ignored, so a keyword may gain
fields.
A line that is malformed (too few fields, an option letter longer than
one character, a long name starting with a dash, an operand number that
is not one) is skipped, silently: completion never prints.
The version changes only for a change that would make an older
.Nm concha
misread a file; a file with a version this
.Nm concha
does not know is passed over, and the search goes on.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa ~/.local/share/concha/complete/
The user's files, when
.Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/concha/complete/
.It Pa /usr/share/concha/complete/
Files installed with programs, when
.Ev XDG_DATA_DIRS
is unset.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The file for a program that runs commands in the background,
.Pa ~/.local/share/concha/complete/tlo :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
complete 1
# tlo(1)
sub  -      start stop log ls bar rm
opt  -      h help
opt  start  b bar     word
arg  start  1         word
arg  start  2         command
opt  log    f follow
arg  log    1         in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tlo
arg  stop   1         in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tlo
.Ed
.Pp
With it,
.Ql "tlo s"
completes to
.Ql start
and
.Ql stop ;
.Ql "tlo start -"
to
.Ql --bar
and
.Ql -b ;
.Ql "tlo start web ech"
to
.Ql echo ,
after which
.Ic echo Ns 's
arguments complete as they would at the prompt; and
.Ql "tlo log "
to the names in
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tlo .
.Pp
To see what a file makes of a line, ask
.Nm concha
for the names
.Ic Tab
would offer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
writeln(completions('tlo log -f '))
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr concha 1 ,
.Xr getopt 3 ,
.Xr concha-tutorial 7
